Chapter: ScopeLetArmGlobal
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A `let` bound inside an `if` arm does not escape the arm,
 so a later read of that name finds whatever was in scope all along --
 here, a GLOBAL of a different type.

 This is the direction that makes the leak visible as a REJECTION rather
 than a miscompile. `env-bind-local` reached the caller's environment
 through `__record-set`, which is an in-place field store returning the
 same record, so the arm's `inner : Integer` stayed bound after the arm
 ended and shadowed the global `inner : Text` at the read. The checker
 then rejected this file with CDX2001 (Text vs Integer) -- a valid
 program the emitter would have compiled correctly, refused by the type
 checker alone.

 `act-let-scope` pins the sibling rule (an act-block let DOES scope over
 the rest of the block) and `arm-local: 23` there pins the emitter's
 half of this one. This file pins the CHECKER's half: the two must agree
 about the same program, and until 2.22 they did not.

Section: Definitions

  inner : Text
  inner = "GLOBAL"

Section: Entry

  arm-scoped : Integer -> Text
  arm-scoped (n) =
   let w = if n > 0 then let inner = n * 10 in inner + 1 else 0
   in inner

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("arm-scoped: " & arm-scoped 2)
  end
